#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7a75441-d233-5a64-8d87-b3138c2d31be" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510900} {:answer/id #uuid "442f019f-9950-54ef-b315-5fed1c784a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510936} {:answer/id #uuid "494e353c-817b-5ca7-a101-f7151bd4954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510944} {:answer/id #uuid "959f6e54-dd4c-5921-b12b-794921892ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511092} {:answer/id #uuid "db09b7e2-e0ce-5f11-ad20-482fba461cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "354ac988-d8c3-57cf-b78f-6912e9ac0585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510900} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b1c6d3-79e8-5764-9d1a-bcce12f2ad8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7a0c5c0-c3dc-5ed9-a9e6-253b15c1791f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161749" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7a75441-d233-5a64-8d87-b3138c2d31be" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e9a4ed-cc02-552b-850f-590a1eabb508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374008} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6da4e6-4d70-5d57-830d-2c3550356100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374009} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c418fd-e2e2-58e2-b16d-b624dda6a4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374010} {:answer/id #uuid "0d81db19-07f6-520d-bd7f-8449d3b4156e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374011} {:answer/id #uuid "91e95a72-f432-5a4d-9e2e-324cee6736c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e95a72-f432-5a4d-9e2e-324cee6736c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374012} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d2f399-c08f-57b6-b31d-d84d8b9705c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "699f2901-2747-5828-8f8f-087336e97be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161754" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d85b11c-498d-53b7-98f8-b742b46e8dc2" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Part-Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1888a529-95b0-58f6-9146-6b50fc190c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510838} {:answer/id #uuid "3756a535-be12-51cb-a4f4-eebb7d355566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510908} {:answer/id #uuid "557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510964} {:answer/id #uuid "5b788302-0dfd-54bf-a49b-6d49283dd409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510974} {:answer/id #uuid "c886c152-17ed-511e-a809-d63c2213db33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "557a8050-44a3-59e1-8a70-aa6bd1dd5460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510964} :answer-field/id #uuid "024d0b3c-4d66-50af-a789-68c23cbe417a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3eee5be8-fdde-5369-b6f0-771204d5bd54" :question/math-academy-id "q-161775" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/e6/e63035cbe368a4f41b7197d70880d0639f004e26996fe6b5055e69966e0f66ee.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1d5b6a-3e7b-567d-a61f-d18f55924912" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510831} {:answer/id #uuid "35312181-3164-564e-951d-368b81aed2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510899} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7493ae-3582-5f1c-82f8-0c2dae976bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510948} {:answer/id #uuid "90ead743-a1fc-5f4f-9075-c4fd3b6866f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511080} {:answer/id #uuid "c72bf197-8bb8-5cea-9e15-63920f550d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1619dc0b-d53c-58c4-92e8-5b68a431da0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510831} :answer-field/id #uuid "60daa8fe-705e-5990-9f0d-a1092f6eb497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6ef3a51-3ce7-571a-8d2a-d26c504b1810" :question/math-academy-id "q-161923" :question/problem "![](images/98/989c9452bebc4ee6c2253c920655830a0b3d0adf0ddb7b90c1ea6a9528ef1448.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ for $y\\le 0,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$  represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=-6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =-6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/df8a9da3b88f3b40d85005b156215f7fa7760257f284acf98ec186d5a2e3ab8d.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function  $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7a75441-d233-5a64-8d87-b3138c2d31be" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aa22172-aed4-5605-92ab-59f3b9c082a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375137} {:answer/id #uuid "47674ef2-eb40-5ead-b7da-b61decf7c865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375138} {:answer/id #uuid "a76f3460-fc95-508d-924f-bca258671c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375139} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0a05b0-be97-5bf2-ae2d-2d653d7ac4e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375140} {:answer/id #uuid "6976b7cc-768c-59b4-88b8-9e266d9b514a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6976b7cc-768c-59b4-88b8-9e266d9b514a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375141} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1c327d-1302-5a46-953f-a8e3268dd875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e21b1a1-dd1a-5b1e-a6f4-aca8044792b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-40073" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
